# XCU sh-pipelines
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XCU.sh-pipelines.001
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.2/connect the standard output
class      required
says       In a pipeline the standard output of each command is the standard
           input of the next.
test       t/001-data-flow.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-pipelines.002
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.2/The exit status of the last
class      required
says       The status of a pipeline is the status of its last command.
test       t/002-status-last.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-pipelines.003
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.2/Begins with
class      required
says       A pipeline that begins with ! has the logical negation of the
           status it would otherwise have: 0 if that was non-zero, else 1.
test       t/003-negation.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-pipelines.004
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.2/pipefail Enabled
class      required
since      2024
says       With pipefail the status is 0 when every command succeeded, else
           the status of the rightmost failing command; with ! the result is
           negated.
test       t/004-pipefail.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-pipelines.005
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.2/The shell shall use the
class      required
since      2024
says       The pipefail setting used is the one in force when the pipeline
           begins: false | set -o pipefail takes its status from the last
           command, not from false.
test       t/005-pipefail-start.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-pipelines.006
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.2/shell shall wait for the last command
class      required
says       The shell waits for the last command of a pipeline to finish
           before going on.
test       t/006-waits-for-last.sh

assertion  XCU.sh-pipelines.007
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.2/The standard
class      required
says       Redirections of a command override the pipe connections made by
           the pipeline: an input redirection replaces the pipe as input, an
           output redirection leaves the next command an empty input.
test       t/007-redirect-after-pipe.sh
